1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,800
The Bumboats and the River
That Was Scrubbed Clean

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:07,818
For more than a century, the
Singapore River was Singapore's

3
00:00:07,898 --> 00:00:11,450
main cargo terminal, and
it never had a proper dock.

4
00:00:11,450 --> 00:00:14,648
Ocean-going ships anchored
out in the roads while

5
00:00:14,728 --> 00:00:18,328
flat-bottomed lighters —
bumboats, twakows, tongkangs —

6
00:00:18,408 --> 00:00:22,141
ferried every sack, crate and
barrel between them and the

7
00:00:22,221 --> 00:00:25,700
godowns lining the riverbank,
load by load, by hand.

8
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:29,361
Singapore's harbour was deep
enough for ocean-going ships,

9
00:00:29,441 --> 00:00:32,199
but the river itself
was shallow and narrow,

10
00:00:32,279 --> 00:00:36,600
too tight for anything bigger than
a lighter to get past its mouth.

11
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:39,973
So ships stayed out at
anchor, and cargo travelled

12
00:00:40,053 --> 00:00:42,321
the last stretch — ship to godown,

13
00:00:42,401 --> 00:00:46,475
godown to ship — in small boats
built for exactly that job.

14
00:00:46,475 --> 00:00:51,169
Early on, the trade belonged to
Chulia lightermen from South India,

15
00:00:51,249 --> 00:00:55,025
using a rounded, double-ended
boat called a tongkang.

16
00:00:55,025 --> 00:00:58,810
By the early 20th century,
Chinese crews — Teochews first,

17
00:00:58,890 --> 00:01:03,008
Hokkiens close behind — had taken
over with a different design,

18
00:01:03,088 --> 00:01:07,605
the twakow: a wide, nearly flat-bottomed
hull, built to carry as much

19
00:01:07,685 --> 00:01:11,950
weight as possible through water
barely deep enough to float it.

20
00:01:11,950 --> 00:01:17,008
A twakow crew was small — two to
four men, usually sharing a dialect,

21
00:01:17,088 --> 00:01:21,252
a surname or a hometown,
working for a towkay who fronted

22
00:01:21,332 --> 00:01:25,725
them advances in hard times
and expected loyalty in return.

23
00:01:25,725 --> 00:01:30,133
The work was exactly as hard as
it looked: cases of dried goods

24
00:01:30,213 --> 00:01:34,478
that could top 200 kilograms,
holds that were "unbearably hot

25
00:01:34,558 --> 00:01:38,975
and humid," and a boat that would
tip if a load went in wrong.

26
00:01:38,975 --> 00:01:43,263
Lightermen were known to work
more than 24 hours straight when

27
00:01:43,343 --> 00:01:47,500
a ship needed clearing, sleeping
in snatches between loads.

28
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:50,825
By 1983, close to 800
lighters were working the

29
00:01:50,905 --> 00:01:54,302
Singapore River and Kallang
Basin between them —

30
00:01:54,382 --> 00:01:58,576
a whole floating economy that
most of the office towers now

31
00:01:58,656 --> 00:02:02,350
standing on the riverbank
have no memory of at all.

32
00:02:02,350 --> 00:02:05,340
I never worked on the
river myself, but I do

33
00:02:05,420 --> 00:02:08,700
have a secondhand link
to the buildings it fed.

34
00:02:08,700 --> 00:02:12,912
I once worked as an FX trader for
Bank of America in Singapore —

35
00:02:12,992 --> 00:02:17,472
one of the founding banks of
Singapore's offshore US-dollar market —

36
00:02:17,552 --> 00:02:20,893
and an older colleague once
showed me a photograph,

37
00:02:20,973 --> 00:02:25,600
his own hair still long in it, of
a godown the bank had once managed.

38
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:29,138
I couldn't say for certain
it was one of these riverside

39
00:02:29,218 --> 00:02:32,304
godowns rather than one
elsewhere in the harbour,

40
00:02:32,384 --> 00:02:35,550
but I recall the river
being visible in the shot.

41
00:02:35,550 --> 00:02:38,256
He'd shown it to me
simply to make a point:

42
00:02:38,336 --> 00:02:41,949
the bank's roots here went
back further than most people,

43
00:02:42,029 --> 00:02:44,750
himself included, ever gave it credit for.

44
00:02:44,750 --> 00:02:49,270
By the 1970s the Singapore River
and the connected Kallang Basin

45
00:02:49,350 --> 00:02:53,798
were catching the runoff of a
fifth of the island's land area —

46
00:02:53,878 --> 00:02:57,975
and most of what flowed into
them had nowhere else to go.

47
00:02:57,975 --> 00:03:03,156
A government count from the period
puts a number on almost everything

48
00:03:03,236 --> 00:03:08,036
that ended up in the water: 27,000
squatter families discharging

49
00:03:08,116 --> 00:03:14,593
sullage straight into the river; 3,600
backyard industries and 430 motor-repair shops;

50
00:03:14,673 --> 00:03:17,109
610 pig farms and 480 duck farms;

51
00:03:17,189 --> 00:03:22,979
4,900 street hawkers washing down beside
open drains; 390 fruit and vegetable

52
00:03:23,059 --> 00:03:28,012
wholesalers dumping spoiled produce;
21,000 premises with no sewer

53
00:03:28,092 --> 00:03:33,200
connection at all, some still on
nightsoil buckets emptied by hand.

54
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:36,890
On top of all of that
sat close to 800 bumboats

55
00:03:36,970 --> 00:03:40,339
and 64 boatyards,
working cargo and repairs

56
00:03:40,419 --> 00:03:44,350
directly over water that
had nowhere to flush to.

57
00:03:44,350 --> 00:03:47,282
The river was, by any practical measure,

58
00:03:47,362 --> 00:03:50,450
an open drain that boats happened to use.

59
00:03:50,450 --> 00:03:54,546
On the 27th of February 1977,
at the opening of an addition

60
00:03:54,626 --> 00:03:58,015
to the Upper Peirce
Reservoir, Prime Minister Lee

61
00:03:58,095 --> 00:04:02,050
Kuan Yew set a public target:
"It should be a way of life

62
00:04:02,130 --> 00:04:05,306
to keep the water clean,
to keep every stream,

63
00:04:05,386 --> 00:04:09,775
every culvert, every rivulet,
free from unnecessary pollution.

64
00:04:09,775 --> 00:04:12,498
In ten years let us have fishing in the

65
00:04:12,578 --> 00:04:16,100
Singapore River and fishing
in the Kallang River.

66
00:04:16,100 --> 00:04:17,900
It can be done."

67
00:04:17,900 --> 00:04:21,619
The Straits Times covered the
same speech the next morning,

68
00:04:21,699 --> 00:04:25,032
though its own headline led
with reservoirs and water

69
00:04:25,112 --> 00:04:28,975
rationing rather than the line
that would end up remembered.

70
00:04:28,975 --> 00:04:32,969
What followed was a genuinely
multi-agency campaign,

71
00:04:33,049 --> 00:04:36,575
run over exactly the
ten years Lee had named.

72
00:04:36,575 --> 00:04:40,417
Squatters, backyard trades
and pig and duck farmers

73
00:04:40,497 --> 00:04:45,494
were resettled into public housing
and proper industrial estates —

74
00:04:45,574 --> 00:04:48,650
more than 26,000 farming families alone.

75
00:04:48,650 --> 00:04:52,070
Street hawkers were moved
into purpose-built food

76
00:04:52,150 --> 00:04:55,650
centres with running water
and sewer connections.

77
00:04:55,650 --> 00:05:00,500
Vegetable wholesalers went to
a new site at Pasir Panjang.

78
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:03,368
Boatyards and the charcoal trade were

79
00:05:03,448 --> 00:05:06,475
phased out or resited to Lorong Halus.

80
00:05:06,475 --> 00:05:10,323
And the bumboats — all 800 of
them — were moved wholesale

81
00:05:10,403 --> 00:05:13,976
out of the Singapore River
and Kallang Basin to a new

82
00:05:14,056 --> 00:05:17,836
lighterage anchorage at Pasir
Panjang, ending, for good,

83
00:05:17,916 --> 00:05:21,775
the trade that had run on
this river for over a century.

84
00:05:21,775 --> 00:05:25,210
By September 1983,
traditional lighterage on

85
00:05:25,290 --> 00:05:28,725
the Singapore River
had effectively ceased.

86
00:05:28,725 --> 00:05:33,140
The nightsoil bucket system,
one of the last holdouts,

87
00:05:33,220 --> 00:05:35,800
was phased out in January 1987.

88
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:39,579
In 1987, Lee marked the
campaign's completion in terms

89
00:05:39,659 --> 00:05:44,510
that read almost like disbelief at
what had actually been pulled off:

90
00:05:44,590 --> 00:05:47,940
"Only ten years ago our
rivers were like sewers,

91
00:05:48,020 --> 00:05:51,950
smelly, dirty and devoid of
fish or any aquatic life...

92
00:05:51,950 --> 00:05:55,048
It was a triumph to
have diverted all toilet

93
00:05:55,128 --> 00:05:59,453
and sullage water into sewers,
and to have resettled hawkers,

94
00:05:59,533 --> 00:06:01,700
boatmen, pig and duck farmers.

95
00:06:01,700 --> 00:06:04,425
We now have pleasant riverscapes.

96
00:06:04,425 --> 00:06:08,145
We can walk along the river
sides and fish or boat,

97
00:06:08,225 --> 00:06:12,100
ski and swim, all unthinkable
only a few years ago."

98
00:06:12,100 --> 00:06:16,200
Fish returned; so did rowing
regattas, a river carnival,

99
00:06:16,280 --> 00:06:18,962
dragon boat races, and eventually the

100
00:06:19,042 --> 00:06:22,475
bars and restaurants
that now line both banks.

101
00:06:22,475 --> 00:06:26,731
I worked in one of those CBD
towers myself for a stretch,

102
00:06:26,811 --> 00:06:31,300
at HSBC, in the years after
the clean-up was long finished.

103
00:06:31,300 --> 00:06:34,259
I used to buy mee pok from a small food

104
00:06:34,339 --> 00:06:37,300
centre by the water on my lunch break.

105
00:06:37,300 --> 00:06:39,960
One day an expat trader took up a bet

106
00:06:40,040 --> 00:06:43,150
with a colleague to swim across the river.

107
00:06:43,150 --> 00:06:46,757
Everyone around us knew, as
a matter of public record,

108
00:06:46,837 --> 00:06:50,717
that the water had been cleaned
— and still felt something

109
00:06:50,797 --> 00:06:54,825
close to revulsion at the idea
of actually getting into it.

110
00:06:54,825 --> 00:06:57,475
He made it across unharmed.

111
00:06:57,475 --> 00:07:00,470
The campaign had succeeded on paper years

112
00:07:00,550 --> 00:07:03,775
before it fully succeeded
in anyone's head.

113
00:07:03,775 --> 00:07:07,723
Why it matters today: the Singapore
River's clean-up gets less

114
00:07:07,803 --> 00:07:10,906
attention than the housing
programme or the port,

115
00:07:10,986 --> 00:07:16,752
maybe because there's no single building to
point to — just water that looks ordinary now,

116
00:07:16,832 --> 00:07:19,950
exactly the way "ordinary"
was the entire point.

117
00:07:19,950 --> 00:07:24,161
But it is one of the clearest
examples of a Singapore pattern:

118
00:07:24,241 --> 00:07:27,968
a hard, unglamorous, decade-long
administrative effort,

119
00:07:28,048 --> 00:07:30,944
aimed at a target that
sounded almost naive

120
00:07:31,024 --> 00:07:34,900
when it was announced, that
actually landed on schedule.

121
00:07:34,900 --> 00:07:38,804
The bumboats that made the river
worth polluting in the first

122
00:07:38,884 --> 00:07:43,115
place are gone from it entirely —
surviving only as tourist boats,

123
00:07:43,195 --> 00:07:47,034
a couple of bronze statues, and
the name "Boat Quay" itself,

124
00:07:47,114 --> 00:07:51,425
now advertising restaurants instead
of the trade it was named for.
